Subject: [PATCH] speech: register the network speech extension when the build has API keys

Every Chrome-branded capture in the corpus reports exactly nineteen
localService:false voices named "Google <language>"; this build reports none.
No profile field can close that, because the voices are not a device property —
they are a capability of the browser build, and patch 0037's `speech.voices`
filter can only constrain voices a provider already registered.

The nineteen are declared in-tree, not invented here:
chrome/browser/resources/network_speech_synthesis/mv3/manifest.json and the MV2
manifest beside it each declare exactly nineteen `"remote": true` voices, and
both manifests are compiled into every build — browser_resources.grd:181-182
includes them outside any `<if>`. The bytes ship; only the registration is
missing.

The registration is gated on branding, at
chrome/browser/extensions/component_loader.cc:642-645:

    // http://crbug.com/41070702
    #if BUILDFLAG(GOOGLE_CHROME_BRANDING) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
      AddNetworkSpeechSynthesisExtension();
    #endif  // BUILDFLAG(GOOGLE_CHROME_BRANDING) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)

But branding is not what makes the voices work. The provider does: the
extension synthesises by posting to https://www.google.com/speech-api/v2/synthesize
with the build's Google API key, which it fetches through
chrome.systemPrivate.getApiKey (network_speech_synthesis/mv3/tts_extension.js:191-192)
— served by
chrome/browser/extensions/api/system_private/system_private_api.cc:134-135,
which returns google_apis::GetAPIKey(). Without a key that is
DefaultApiKeys::kUnsetApiToken and every request fails.

So this replaces the branding gate with the condition that actually decides
whether the voices can speak, rather than deleting it. HasAPIKeyConfigured() is
Chromium's own predicate for "this build has a real key" — literally
`api_key_ != kUnsetApiToken` (google_apis/api_key_cache.cc:341), reached through
google_apis/google_api_keys.cc:73-75 — and Chromium already uses it exactly this
way to decide whether a feature is available at all, in
components/translate/core/browser/translate_manager.cc:177 and
chrome/browser/ui/startup/infobar_utils.cc:188.

The consequence for a keyless build is deliberate: the extension is not loaded,
and the voice list is exactly what it is today. Patch 0043 forbids listing a
voice that cannot speak, and speak() is a probe a page can actually run, so
nineteen listed-but-failing voices would be a worse result than nineteen
missing ones — a wrong answer to a cheap question rather than an honest answer
to an expensive one.

Keys are supplied at configure time through build/args/common.gni, which reads
GOOGLE_API_KEY, GOOGLE_DEFAULT_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_DEFAULT_CLIENT_SECRET from
the environment so nothing is ever committed. The operator obtains them under
docs/api_keys.md and is responsible for the ToS they accepted; the consequences
of supplying them are written out in build/args/common.gni beside the arg.

Note what is *not* claimed: a configured key proves a key exists, not that it is
authorised for the speech endpoint. An operator whose key lacks that access
would list nineteen voices that error on speak. Whoever configures keys must
verify one utterance actually produces audio before shipping that build; no
in-binary check can substitute, because the only honest test is a network round
trip and making one at startup would add an observable of its own.

Loading the extension also removes the "You are missing API keys" infobar
(chrome/browser/ui/startup/infobar_utils.cc:188-190), which today steals window
chrome height from every launch — the same surface coh.chrome-inset-vs-infobar-state
covers.

Remote voices remain subject to the connection type: TtsControllerImpl clears
them on cellular and offline connections (content/browser/speech/tts_controller_impl.cc:1023-1042).
That is upstream Chrome behaviour and a real Chrome on the same connection
behaves identically, so it is inherited rather than patched.

Ledger: speech.voices, coh.platform-resource-sets.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/extensions/component_loader.cc b/chrome/browser/extensions/component_loader.cc
index e047fdc..0ddb088 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/extensions/component_loader.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/extensions/component_loader.cc
@@ -58,6 +58,7 @@
 #include "extensions/common/extension_l10n_util.h"
 #include "extensions/common/file_util.h"
 #include "extensions/common/manifest_constants.h"
+#include "google_apis/google_api_keys.h"
 #include "pdf/buildflags.h"
 #include "printing/buildflags/buildflags.h"
 #include "ui/accessibility/accessibility_features.h"
@@ -638,9 +639,23 @@ void ComponentLoader::AddDefaultComponentExtensionsWithBackgroundPages(
   AddAimEligibilityExtension();
   AddGlicExtension();
   AddContextualTasksExtension();
 
-// http://crbug.com/41070702
-#if BUILDFLAG(GOOGLE_CHROME_BRANDING) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
-  AddNetworkSpeechSynthesisExtension();
-#endif  // BUILDFLAG(GOOGLE_CHROME_BRANDING) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+// Apostate: upstream compiles this call out of every non-Chrome-branded build
+// (http://crbug.com/41070702), which is why this binary reports zero
+// localService:false voices where every Chrome capture reports nineteen. The
+// extension's own bytes ship regardless — its manifests are included
+// unconditionally by browser_resources.grd — so only the registration was
+// missing.
+//
+// What decides whether those voices can speak is the API key, not the
+// branding: the extension posts to the Google speech endpoint with the key it
+// fetches through chrome.systemPrivate.getApiKey. Gating on the key keeps a
+// keyless build exactly as honest as it is today — no voices listed, none
+// promised — and lets a build configured with keys present the voices it can
+// really serve. Listing a voice that cannot speak is what patch 0043 forbids.
+#if !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+  if (google_apis::HasAPIKeyConfigured()) {
+    AddNetworkSpeechSynthesisExtension();
+  }
+#endif  // !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
 }
